  "final_answer": "The polynomial can be written with far fewer terms as a polynomial in (x − 4):\n\n**x⁵ − 20x⁴ + 163x³ − 676x² + 1424x − 1209 = (x − 4)⁵ + 3(x − 4)³ + 7**\n\nThat's 3 terms instead of 6. I verified it on Wolfram|Alpha by expanding (x − 4)⁵ + 3(x − 4)³ + 7, which returns exactly x^5 − 20x^4 + 163x^3 − 676x^2 + 1424x − 1209 (6 terms).\n\nFor context, Wolfram|Alpha's factorization over the integers gives nothing nicer — the quintic has one real root (≈2.82987) and two complex-conjugate pairs, so it doesn't factor into rational-coefficient linear/quadratic pieces. The shifted-variable form above is the compact \"fewer items\" version.",
